      "data": {
        "text": "**Considering Audio Streaming**\n\nI'm thinking about how to build `stream_chunks.py`. My focus is on sourcing audio chunks asynchronously. The main task will be to pick a specific external provider and ensure it's clearly defined in the code. I'm considering audio processing frameworks for the async streaming component.\n\n\n**Defining Cloud Provider**\n\nI've decided on Google Cloud Speech-to-Text as the concrete external provider, ensuring easy identification in the code with its client library. I'm structuring the `stream_chunks.py` file to import the Google Cloud client and define async functions. The plan is to have a generator for audio chunks and a separate function to stream these chunks to the Google API. I'm focusing on incorporating provider-specific elements like `speech_v1.StreamingRecognitionConfig`.\n\n\n**Structuring the Code**\n\nI'm now outlining `stream_chunks.py`, with imports for Google Cloud Speech-to-Text (`google.cloud.speech_v1`) and `asyncio`. I'll build an async generator, `_generate_audio_chunks()`, for simulated audio, and an async function, `stream_audio_to_google_cloud()`. This function will create a `speech.SpeechAsyncClient`, configure `RecognitionConfig` and `StreamingRecognitionConfig`, then stream audio chunks to Google's API via `client.streaming_recognize()`, iterating and printing responses.\n\n\n**Confirming API Access**\n\nI'm now cross-referencing my understanding of Google Cloud Speech-to-Text. I need to ensure that I have the correct imports, focusing on `google.cloud.speech_v1` and the async client. This includes verifying the initialization process for the async client and confirming how to structure the recognition configuration, including the audio encoding, sample rate, and language code. My goal is to streamline the call to `streaming_recognize`.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789864966621,
          "start": 1789864961417
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0bc43e5890017VuIe3A05QSQje",
